MAME ds1386: load the day-of-month register from `day`, not from `second`.

THE BUG

`ds1386_device::rtc_clock_updated()` is the device_rtc_interface callback MAME
uses to seed a battery-backed RTC from the host clock. It assigns:

    m_days           = make_bcd(day_of_week);
    m_date           = make_bcd(second);     // <-- day-of-month from SECONDS
    m_months_enables = ...make_bcd(month)...;
    m_years          = make_bcd(year);

The `day` parameter is never used anywhere in the function. `m_date` is the
day-of-month register, and it is being loaded with the seconds field, so the
emulated machine's day-of-month is whatever second of the minute MAME happened
to start on.

WHY IT MATTERED HERE (SGI Indy / IRIX 6.5 exhibit)

The tile's boots were intermittently and inexplicably nondeterministic: a
black-screen boot hang appeared at ~7% with no discoverable trigger, hang rates
varied a lot between measurement sessions, and retrying a failed boot usually
worked. Reading the guest's own `/var/adm/SYSLOG` out of preserved write
overlays showed why -- boots started from BYTE-IDENTICAL nvram reported dates
years apart:

    golden (as built)   Jul  3 23:47
    healthy trial       Jul 12 03:00
    hung trial          Aug 16 06:01
    live tile           Aug 24 03:59

None matched the host date, and none matched the date deliberately baked into
the golden nvram by the install procedure (`date 0730120026`). Two separate
things were going on, and both are worth knowing:

  1. `running_machine::start()` calls `set_rtc_datetime()` AFTER `nvram_load()`,
     with the comment "this may overwrite parts of NVRAM". For a device that
     does not override `rtc_battery_backed()` -- ds1386 does not -- the
     nvram-restored clock is therefore always overwritten by the host clock. A
     date baked into nvram is silently discarded.
  2. This bug then scrambles the result, putting the host's SECONDS into the
     guest's day-of-month, which is what makes the guest date jump around from
     one launch to the next.

IRIX startup branches on time in several places, so a per-launch pseudo-random
date is a real nondeterminism source in a boot everyone had assumed was
deterministic.

WORKAROUND WITHOUT PATCHING

MAME's `-rtc YYYYMMDDhhmmss` option pins `m_base_time`, which makes the seeding
deterministic even with the bug present. Verified: two independent boots with
`-rtc 19960704120000` both reported `Jul 12 03:00:18` -- identical to the
second, where unpinned boots differed by weeks. (Note the guest reads back
Jul *12*, not the Jul *4* requested: that is this bug, deterministically.)

Apply from the MAME source root:
  patch -p1 -d src/devices/machine < mame-ds1386-date-from-day.patch

--- a/ds1386.cpp
+++ b/ds1386.cpp
@@ -110,7 +110,7 @@
 	else
 		m_hours = time_helper::make_bcd(hour);
 	m_days = time_helper::make_bcd(day_of_week);
-	m_date = time_helper::make_bcd(second);
+	m_date = time_helper::make_bcd(day);
 	m_months_enables = (time_helper::make_bcd(month) & 0x1f) | (m_months_enables & 0xc0);
 	m_years = time_helper::make_bcd(year);
 
